    <h2>What a Good Marketing Agency Actually Does</h2>

    <p>A good agency is not just "doing your social media." It builds a system that turns attention into orders and first orders into repeat orders. Here is what that looks like:</p>

    <h3>Strategy First</h3>
    <p>Before creating a single post or running a single ad, a good agency learns your brand: who your buyers are, what makes the product different, your margins, where the openings are and what will actually move sales. Cookie-cutter marketing does not work.</p>

    <h3>Execution That Compounds</h3>
    <p>Every winning ad teaches you about your buyer. A good agency checks whether each email and page change improves the customer action, keeps what helps and looks into what does not. A good agency builds systems where each piece of work makes the next one more effective. Over time, you should own an asset, not just a pile of posts.</p>

    <h3>Measurement and Optimization</h3>
    <p>You should know exactly what your marketing spend is producing. How many orders came in this month? What did each new customer cost? How many came back to buy again? Which channels are working? If your agency cannot answer these with data, it is not doing its job.</p>

    <h2>Red Flags When Evaluating Agencies</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>"We guarantee results."</strong> Nobody can guarantee a ROAS number or a #1 ranking. If they promise it, they are either guessing or cutting corners.</li>
      <li><strong>Long-term contracts with no exit clause.</strong> If their work is good, you will not want to leave. If it is not, you should not be locked in.</li>
      <li><strong>No case studies or references.</strong> If they cannot show real results from real clients, that is a problem.</li>
      <li><strong>Vague reporting.</strong> "Engagement is up!" means nothing. You need specific numbers tied to sales and profit.</li>
      <li><strong>One-size-fits-all packages.</strong> Your brand is not like every other brand. An agency that sells the exact same package to every client is running a template, not building a strategy.</li>
      <li><strong>They never say no.</strong> A good agency pushes back when an idea will not work and suggests something better. If they agree to everything, they are order takers, not strategists.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Green Flags That Signal a Good Agency</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>They ask more questions than they pitch.</strong> The first conversation should be mostly about your brand, not their services.</li>
      <li><strong>Documented results.</strong> <a href="/results/">Real client work</a> they can walk you through, not vague claims like "increased brand awareness."</li>
      <li><strong>Transparent reporting.</strong> Reports you can actually understand, with numbers tied to revenue.</li>
      <li><strong>They explain the why.</strong> You should understand why they recommend an approach, not just what they are doing.</li>
      <li><strong>Responsive communication.</strong> If it takes them three days to answer an email while they are trying to win your business, imagine how slow they will be once you are a client.</li>
      <li><strong>They understand DTC.</strong> Or they are honest about what they do not know and show how they will get up to speed fast.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Hybrid Approach: Founder Plus Agency</h2>

    <p>A mix often works well: the founder handles what needs the founder's voice (filming, stories, relationships with creators and customers), while the agency handles the technical and strategic work (ads, tracking, automation, <a href="/services/social-media-management/">scheduling</a>, analytics, <a href="/services/website-design/">landing page optimization</a>).</p>

    <p>That is how we like to work. You bring the product and the personality. We build the system that puts it in front of the right buyers and turns attention into revenue.</p>

    <h2>How to Know You're Ready</h2>

    <p>You are ready to hire a marketing agency when:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Marketing is costing you sales.</strong> Ads you cannot read. A store that does not convert. Customers who buy once and disappear.</li>
      <li><strong>Your product sells when people find it, but not enough people are finding it.</strong> This is the clearest signal. The product works. You need more of the right buyers to see it.</li>
      <li><strong>You are spending your time on marketing instead of the business.</strong> Every hour you spend editing ads is an hour you are not spending on product, partners or operations.</li>
      <li><strong>You have taken DIY as far as it goes.</strong> You did what you could. Now you need the next level of expertise.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>How much does it cost to hire a marketing agency?</h3>
    <p>Agency cost follows scope: which channels the agency runs, how much creative and tracking work is involved, and how reporting works. Tools and ad spend may sit outside the agreement. Compare proposals against the same business problem and reporting requirements, and ask each agency for a written scope before you sign.</p>

    <h3>Who should own the accounts and data when I hire an agency?</h3>
    <p>Your brand should keep ownership of its ad accounts, analytics, store, domain and customer data. Give an agency the access required for the agreed work. Ask how creative files, reports and documentation will be handed over, and make sure the exit process is clear before signing.</p>

    <h3>How should I judge an agency before expanding its scope?</h3>
    <p>Judge an agency against the problem it was hired to solve and an agreed starting point. Review completed work, data quality, customer acquisition, repeat orders and contribution after relevant costs. Ask what changed, what remains uncertain and what the team will test next before expanding the scope.</p>

